    <node id="Agave">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">Agave americana</data>
      <data key="d2">American Agave | American Aloe | Amerikanische Agave | Century Plant | Garingboom | Hundertjährige Agave | Maguey | Pita Común | Pite | Spreading Century Plant</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Agave</data>
      <data key="d4">Agave is a succulent plant commonly found in the southwestern US, as well as some parts of Mexico, Central and South America, the Mediterranean, and India. Agave has thick, hard, rigid leaves that arise from a rosette. The leaves are lined with curved spines with spikes similar to fishhooks .</data>
      <data key="d5">⚠️ AVOID: Likely Unsafe when used orally. Agave contains constituents that may be uterine stimulants, and it has traditionally been used to induce labor .</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Agmatine">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1-(4-aminobutyl)guanidine | Agmatin | Agmatina | Agmatine Sulfate | Decarboxylated Arginine</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Agmatine</data>
      <data key="d4">Agmatine is an endogenous polyamine found in bacteria, plants, and animals, including humans. It is synthesized from the amino acid arginine by arginine decarboxylase .</data>
      <data key="d5">⚠️ AVOID: Insufficient reliable information available; avoid using.</data>
