    <node id="Blue-Green Algae">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">Anabaena species</data>
      <data key="d2">AFA | Algae | Algas Verdiazul | Algue Bleu-Vert | Algues Bleu-Vert | Algues Bleu-Vert du Lac Klamath | BGA | Blue Green Algae | Blue Green Algas | Blue-Green Micro-Algae</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Blue-Green-Algae</data>
      <data key="d4">The term "blue-green algae" refers to several aquatic and photosynthetic prokaryotic bacterial species of the phylum Cyanobacteria . The most common species of blue-green algae found in supplements are from the Arthrospira genus, and referred to as spirulina . Another common species of blue-green al</data>
      <data key="d5">⚠️ AVOID: Insufficient reliable information available; avoid using. Some blue-green algae products, specifically those of the species Aphanizomenon flos-aquae, have been found to contain low amounts of beta-methylamino-L-alanine (BMAA). BMAA is associated with neurodegenerative diseases, and breast milk has been shown to be a potential source of BMAA exposure in infants .</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bog Bilberry">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">Vaccinium uliginosum</data>
      <data key="d2">Airelle Bleue | Airelle des Marais | Airelle des marais | Airelle des Marécages | Airelle Noire | Arándano de Pantano | Arándano Negro | Embrune | Fausse Myrtille | Moosbeere</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Bog-Bilberry</data>
      <data key="d4">Bog bilberry is a plant with small blue fruit similar to blueberries that grows in cooler areas of the Northern Hemisphere. The berries are eaten as food and used as medicine . It has traditionally been used for gastrointestinal inflammation and bladder complaints.</data>
      <data key="d5">✅ SAFE: Possibly Safe when the fruit is used orally in food amounts .</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
