    <node id="Broccoli Sprout">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">Brassica oleracea</data>
      <data key="d2">Brotes de brócoli | Pousse de brocoli | Sprouting Broccoli</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Broccoli-Sprout</data>
      <data key="d4">Broccoli sprouts are young broccoli plants (3-4 days old) that resemble alfalfa sprouts but have a more bitter flavor. Broccoli sprouts are rich in glucoraphanin, which is metabolized to sulforaphane during digestion . Broccoli sprouts contains 20 times as much glucoraphanin as mature broccoli .</data>
      <data key="d5">✅ SAFE: Likely Safe when COOKED broccoli sprouts are consumed in amounts commonly found in foods. Broccoli sprout is considered safe for dietary consumption provided the sprouts are grown in accordance with food safety recommendations developed by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and are cooked prior to eating .</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bromelain">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">Bromelaine | Bromélaïne | Bromelains | Bromelainum | Bromelin | Bromelina | Broméline | Enzyme d'Ananas | Pineapple Enzyme | Pineapple Frui t Bromelain</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Bromelain</data>
      <data key="d4">Bromelain is a general name for proteolytic enzymes obtained from the stem and fruit of the pineapple .</data>
