    <node id="Castor Bean">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">Ricinus communis</data>
      <data key="d2">African Coffee Tree | Arandi | Bi Ma Zi | Bofareira | Castorbean | Erand | Eranda | Gandharva Hasta | Graine de Ricin | Graine de ricin</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Castor-Bean</data>
      <data key="d4">Castor oil is the oil obtained from the castor bean. Its taste is initially bland, followed by a slightly bitter and usually nauseating taste. Castor oil is sometimes flavored with cinnamon, peppermint, or other flavorings to mask the unpleasant taste . Traditionally, castor oil has been used as a l</data>
      <data key="d5">✅ SAFE: Possibly Safe when castor oil is used orally in pregnant women at term. Although there have been reports of fetal meconium passage being more common with castor oil use in pregnant women , clinical research shows that the incidence of fetal meconium passage does not significantly differ from no castor oil use . Midwives routinely use castor oil for labor induction in pregnant women at term. This practice does not appear to adversely affect the mother or fetus . However, castor oil should not be</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
