    <node id="Lactase">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">Beta-galactosidase | Bêta-Galactosidase | Lactasa</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Lactase</data>
      <data key="d4">Lactase is an enzyme that breaks down lactose into glucose and galactose . Some people are "lactose intolerant" and are unable to break down lactose on their own. This can cause cramps, diarrhea, and gas, and can result in avoidance of milk and dairy products . Commercially available lactase is deri</data>
      <data key="d5">✅ SAFE: Likely Safe when used orally and appropriately with lactose-containing foods.</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lactic Acid">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">Acide 2-hydroxypropionique (Acide Lactique) | Acide Hydroxypropionique | Acide Lactique | Acide lactique | Ácido láctico | Hydroxypropionic Acid | Milk Acid</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Lactic-Acid</data>
      <data key="d4">Lactic acid is a type of alpha hydroxy acid. Alpha hydroxy acids are natural acids found in foods, including, but not limited to, citrus fruits, sugarcane, sour milk, apples, and grapes. Alpha hydroxy acids are commonly used in cosmetic products . Lactic acid is sometimes found as an active ingredie</data>
      <data key="d5">⚠️ AVOID: Likely Safe when used topically in appropriate concentrations . There is insufficient reliable information available about the safety of lactic acid when used orally during pregnancy and lactation; avoid using.</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lacticaseibacillus Casei">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">Lacticaseibacillus casei</data>
      <data key="d2">L. casei | Lactobacillus Casei | Lactobacillus Zeae | Probiotic | Probiotique</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Lacticaseibacillus-casei</data>
      <data key="d4">Lacticaseibacillus casei is a species of lactic acid-producing, gram-positive, rod-shaped, homofermentive, anaerobic bacteria. L. casei is found in infant feces and the nasopharynx. Specific strains of L. casei are found as probiotics in supplements and functional foods. Probiotics are live microbes</data>
      <data key="d5">✅ SAFE: Possibly Safe when used orally and appropriately. A combination of Lacticaseibacillus casei, Lactobacillus acidophilus, and Bifidobacterium bifidum has been used with apparent safety for 6 weeks, starting at 24-28 weeks' gestation .</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
