    <node id="N-Acetyl Cysteine (Nac)">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">Acetyl Cysteine | Acétyl Cystéine | Acetylcysteine | Acétylcystéine | NAC | N-Acetil Cisteína | N-acetilcisteína (NAC) | N-Acétyl Cystéine | N-Acetyl-B-Cysteine | N-Acetylcysteine</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/NAcetyl-Cysteine-NAC</data>
      <data key="d4">N-acetyl cysteine (NAC) is a derivative of the amino acid L-cysteine. In the body, it is a source of sulfhydryl (-SH) groups, which allows it to act as a potent antioxidant. Also, N-acetyl cysteine increases glutathione levels in the body; glutathione deficiency is associated with a variety of patho</data>
      <data key="d5">✅ SAFE: Possibly Safe when used orally, intratracheally, intravenously, or by inhalation. N-acetyl cysteine crosses the placenta, but has not been associated with adverse effects to the fetus . However, N-acetyl cysteine should only be used in pregnancy when clearly indicated, such as in cases of acetaminophen toxicity.</data>
