      <data key="d2">Absinthe | Absinthe Suisse | Absinthii Herba | Absinthites | Afstantin | Ajenjo | Alvine | Armoise | Armoise Absinthe | Armoise Amère</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Wormwood</data>
      <data key="d4">Wormwood is a perennial bush with aromatic leaves and a bitter taste . Wormwood is a component of the alcoholic beverages vermouth and absinthe . Orally, wormwood has traditionally been used as an anthelmintic, antipyretic, antispasmodic, aphrodisiac, appetite stimulant, diaphoretic, and tonic. Topi</data>
      <data key="d5">⚠️ AVOID: LIKELY UNSAFE ..when used orally in amounts greater than those found in foods . Some wormwood products contain thujone, a neurotoxin. Theoretically, thujone also has potential uterine and menstrual stimulant effects . There is insufficient reliable information available about the safety of wormwood when used topically during pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
