    <node id="Yerba Mate">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">Ilex paraguariensis</data>
      <data key="d2">Chimarrao | Green Mate | Hervea | Ilex | Jesuit's Brazil Tea | Jesuit's Tea | Maté | Maté Folium | Paraguay Tea | St. Bartholemew's Tea</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Yerba-Mate</data>
      <data key="d4">Yerba mate, also known as mate or Yerba Maté, is a popular beverage, much like coffee or tea . It is prepared from the leaves of the Ilex paraguariensis tree and is traditionally consumed in Latin and South America . It has been traditionally used as a laxative and diuretic and to alter mood or affe</data>
      <data key="d5">⚠️ AVOID: Possibly Unsafe when used orally. Yerba mate is associated with an increased risk of cancer, including esophageal, kidney, bladder, cervical, prostate, lung, renal cell, and possibly mouth and laryngeal cancer . However, teratogenic studies have not been performed. Yerba mate also contains caffeine. Caffeine crosses the placenta, producing fetal blood concentrations similar to parental levels. According to a review by Health Canada, and a subsequent large meta-analysis conducted in the US, most</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Yerba Santa">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">Eriodictyon angustifolium</data>
      <data key="d2">Bear's Weed | Consumptive's Weed | Eriodictyon | Gum Bush | Gum Plant | Herbe à Ourse | Herbe des Montagnes | Herbe Sacrée | Herbe Sainte | Hierba Santa</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Yerba-Santa</data>
      <data key="d4">Yerba santa is a shrub native to North America and traditionally used in Native American medicine for respiratory conditions, fever, bruises, infections, insect bites, and headache .</data>
      <data key="d5">⚠️ AVOID: Insufficient reliable information available; avoid using.</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Yew">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">Arbre Sacré des Druides | Chinwood | Common Yew | English Yew | European Yew | Himalayan Yew | If | If à Baies | If Commun | If de l'Himalaya</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Yew</data>
      <data key="d4">Yew is a type of evergreen tree or bush . It is grown mainly for ornamental purposes. It is also used medicinally . The leaves, or needles, and seeds are highly poisonous due to the presence of taxine alkaloids, which are found throughout the plant .</data>
      <data key="d5">ℹ️ No pregnancy data available. Consult your physician.</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Yin Chen">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">Artemisia capillaris</data>
      <data key="d2">Armoise à Balais | Armoise Capillaire | Armoise yin-chen | Artemisia Officinalis | Capillary Wormwood | Ceinture de Saint-Jean | Chiu | Couronne de Saint-Jean | Herbe à Cent Goûts | In Chen</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/Yin-Chen</data>
      <data key="d4">Yin chen can be sourced from two different species of Artemisia: A. scoparia and A. capillaris. A. scoparia is a perennial plant found in sandy coastal areas of Asia. It has a long history of use in Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) .</data>
      <data key="d5">⚠️ AVOID: Insufficient reliable information available; avoid using.</data>
