      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">19-N-Andro | 19-nor-4-androstene-3 | 17-dione | 19-nor-A-dione | 19-Norandrost-4-ene-3 | 17-dione | 19-Norandrostenedione | 19nor-dehydroepiandrosterone | 19nor-DHEA | Estr-4-ene-3</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/19-nor-DHEA</data>
      <data key="d4">19-nor-DHEA is marketed as a prohormone, or a hormone precursor. Usually, a segment of the prohormone is cleaved or altered to produce a biologically active hormone such as testosterone, estrogen, or a derivative . NOTE : This is a Fast Facts monograph, intended to give subscribers quick information</data>
      <data key="d5">⚕️ CONSULT PHYSICIAN: Insufficient Reliable Evidence to Rate</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="1-Androsterone">
      <data key="d0">supplement</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1-Androsten-3beta-ol-17-one | 1-Androstene-3beta-ol | 17-one | 1-Androstène-3bêta-ol | 17-one | 1-Androstene-3beta-ol-17-one | 1-Androstène-3bêta-ol-17-one | 1-Androstène-3bêta-ol-17-one | 1-Androsterona | 1-Androstérone</data>
      <data key="d3">https://naturalmedicines.therapeuticresearch.com/Data/ProMonographs/1-Androsterone</data>
      <data key="d4">1-Androsterone is a prohormone that is included as an ingredient in a variety of supplements marketed to athletes and bodybuilders. Prohormones are typically designed to be metabolized to anabolic compounds . Although there is very little known about these compounds, they may cause serious anabolic</data>
      <data key="d5">⚕️ CONSULT PHYSICIAN: or nursing infants.</data>
