    <edge source="Acetyl-L-Carnitine" target="Serotonergic Drugs">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">SEROTONERGIC DRUGS: Interaction Rating Moderate Be cautious with this combination. Severity HIGH Occurrence POSSIBLE Level of Evidence D (In vitro or animal study) Theoretically, acetyl-L-carnitine mi</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acetyl-L-Carnitine" target="Thyroid Hormone">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">THYROID HORMONE: Interaction Rating Moderate Be cautious with this combination. Severity MODERATE Occurrence PROBABLE Level of Evidence B (Lower quality RCT) Theoretically, acetyl-L-carnitine might de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acetyl-L-Carnitine" target="D-Carnitine">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">D-CARNITINE: Theoretically, the transport of acetyl-L-carnitine might be inhibited by D-carnitine. Some evidence suggests that D-carnitine competes with L-carnitine in active transport systems. Taking</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acetyl-L-Carnitine" target="Herbs And Supplements With Serotonergic Properties">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">HERBS AND SUPPLEMENTS WITH SEROTONERGIC PROPERTIES: Theoretically, acetyl-L-carnitine might have serotonergic effects. Taking acetyl-L-carnitine with other products with serotonergic properties might </data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acetyl-L-Carnitine" target="Bipolar Disorder">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">BIPOLAR DISORDER: Case reports suggest that acetyl-L-carnitine might increase the risk of mania in patients with bipolar disorder ; use with caution in patients with bipolar disorder.</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acetyl-L-Carnitine" target="Chemotherapy-Induced Peripheral Neuropathy">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">CHEMOTHERAPY-INDUCED PERIPHERAL NEUROPATHY: Using acetyl-L-carnitine in patients undergoing taxane-based chemotherapy may increase symptoms of neuropathy ; avoid use for the prevention of chemotherapy</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acetyl-L-Carnitine" target="Hypothyroidism">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">HYPOTHYROIDISM: Theoretically, acetyl-L-carnitine might interfere with the activity of thyroid hormone in the body. This is based on evidence that L-carnitine seems to inhibit the activity of thyroid </data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acetyl-L-Carnitine" target="Seizures">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">SEIZURES: Theoretically, acetyl-L-carnitine might increase the risk for seizures in patients with seizure disorders. This is based on reports of an increase in seizure frequency or severity in patient</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acetyl-L-Carnitine" target="Besifovir">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">BESIFOVIR: Depletion Rating Moderate Monitor for depletion; a supplement is needed in some patients. Theoretically, acetyl-L-carnitine levels may be reduced in patients taking besifovir.</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acetyl-L-Carnitine" target="Sulfadiazine">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">SULFADIAZINE: Depletion Rating Insufficient Evidence Clinical significance is not known. Theoretically, acetyl-L-carnitine levels may be reduced in patients taking sulfadiazine.</data>
    </edge>
