    <edge source="Potassium" target="Kidney Disease">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">KIDNEY DISEASE: Kidney disease affects potassium excretion and increases the risk for elevated potassium levels. Additionally, patients on hemodialysis may experience fluctuations in potassium levels.</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Potassium" target="Beta-2 Agonists">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">BETA-2 AGONISTS: Depletion Rating Moderate Monitor for depletion; a supplement is needed in some patients. Beta-2 agonists can reduce serum levels of potassium.</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Potassium" target="Corticosteroids">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">CORTICOSTEROIDS: Depletion Rating Moderate Monitor for depletion; a supplement is needed in some patients. Corticosteroids can increase urinary potassium excretion.</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Potassium" target="Diuretic Drugs">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">DIURETIC DRUGS: Depletion Rating Major A supplement is needed for most patients. Some diuretic drugs increase urinary potassium excretion.</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Potassium" target="Levodopa">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">LEVODOPA: Depletion Rating Insignificant A supplement is not needed for most patients. Levodopa can increase potassium excretion, but this is unlikely to be clinically relevant.</data>
    </edge>
