    <edge source="Folic Acid" target="5-Fluorouracil">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">5-FLUOROURACIL: Interaction Rating Moderate Be cautious with this combination. Severity HIGH Occurrence POSSIBLE Level of Evidence D (Theoretical based on pharmacology) Theoretically, high doses of fo</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Folic Acid" target="Green Tea">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">GREEN TEA: Theoretically, green tea might decrease the activity of folic acid. In vitro, the green tea constituent, epigallocatechin gallate (EGCG), appears to inhibit the activity of the enzyme dihyd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Folic Acid" target="Zinc">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">ZINC: Theoretically, folic acid might reduce the absorption of zinc. There is concern that folic acid might reduce the absorption of zinc. However, evidence on the effects of supplemental folic acid o</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Folic Acid" target="Angioplasty">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">ANGIOPLASTY: There is some concern that B vitamins might increase the rate of restenosis after bare metal stent placement. An intravenous loading dose of folic acid, vitamin B6 and vitamin B12, follow</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Folic Acid" target="Cancer">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">CANCER: There is some concern that folic acid might increase the risk of certain cancers. Preliminary clinical research suggests folic acid in doses of 0.8-1 mg daily might increase the risk of cancer</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Folic Acid" target="Seizure Disorders">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">SEIZURE DISORDERS: High supplemental doses of folic acid might exacerbate seizures in people with seizure disorders. Doses less than 1 mg have rarely been associated with increased seizure activity .</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Folic Acid" target="Vitamin B12 Deficiency">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">VITAMIN B12 DEFICIENCY: There is some concern that folic acid can improve hematologic signs of megaloblastic anemia, without resolving the underlying cause, thereby masking vitamin B12 deficiency. Thi</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Folic Acid" target="Aminosalicylic Acid">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">AMINOSALICYLIC ACID: Depletion Rating Moderate Monitor for depletion; a supplement is needed in some patients. Aminosalicylic acid might reduce serum folate levels and increase the risk of folate defi</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Folic Acid" target="Antacids">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">ANTACIDS: Depletion Rating Insignificant A supplement is not needed for most patients. Theoretically, antacids might reduce folate absorption; however, this is not likely to be clinically significant.</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Folic Acid" target="Antibiotic Drugs">
      <data key="d7">INTERACTS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d8">0.9</data>
      <data key="d9">ANTIBIOTIC DRUGS: Depletion Rating Insignificant A supplement is not needed for most patients. Theoretically, antibiotics might reduce folate absorption; however, this is not likely to be clinically s</data>
    </edge>
